                        "form": "ensetes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "780512e2-6a55-3b8e-9afb-d04200005031",
                        "entry_id": "a2dd8dd4-6597-3dd1-9891-c3e9ec3a2774",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Ensete ventricosum, a species of flowering plant in the banana family Musaceae, the root of which is used for food and other purposes."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f5a97a63-8fd0-403d-bedc-b40ba17e2199",
                                "sense_id": "780512e2-6a55-3b8e-9afb-d04200005031",
                                "text": "[T]he figs of the Enſete are not eatable; they are of a tender, ſoft ſubstance; watery, taſteleſs, and in colour and conſiſtence ſimilar to a rotten apricot; they are of a conical form, crooked a little at the lower end, about an inch and a half in length, and an inch in breadth where thickeſt."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dbfd9795-2238-4892-8424-8c34c41cd6ee",
                                "sense_id": "780512e2-6a55-3b8e-9afb-d04200005031",
                                "text": "Were it not for the enſete, therefore, the Galla would have ſcarce any vegetable food. Mr [James] Bruce thinks that the enſete may have been cultivated in ſome of the gardens of Egypt about Roſetto, but that it was not a native of the country."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0e47ea26-7759-4321-a0cb-375acd88e1f0",
                                "sense_id": "780512e2-6a55-3b8e-9afb-d04200005031",
                                "text": "On the way I saw, for the first time, Enset, a nice plant, which is described in [James] Bruce's Travels. The Abyssinians use it in baking bread, which is wrapped in it, and gives to it a particular scent, which I do not like."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "87379c2c-f939-460a-8bec-a8513b390a2f",
                                "sense_id": "780512e2-6a55-3b8e-9afb-d04200005031",
                                "text": "Here, among his Cycads and his Orchids, his Ensetes and his Dracænas, spade in hand, a wideawake hat on his head, a rare flower in his button-hole, and rustic contentment irradiating his face—here, amid scenery which typifies a botanist's paradise, Sir John Kirk is emphatically At Home."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d5113e87-f9df-40ee-b2a8-9e8144d9f11f",
                                "sense_id": "780512e2-6a55-3b8e-9afb-d04200005031",
                                "text": "Another instance of religious influence on diversity of cultivars is found in Ethiopia, where the Ari people cultivate ensete, a relative of the banana (Shigeta 1990). Ensete leaves are used for wrapping, thatching, ground cover for sitting, containers, dresses, ornamentation at funerals and weddings, and shading from sun and rain."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
